(function(root){'use strict';var L=root.L,A={"topics": [{"id": "language-modes", "title": "Communication approaches", "summary": "Compare listening, visual communication, and family-centered choices.", "part": "Language and Speech · Chapter 6", "sectionId": "e3-language-modes", "concepts": ["e3-ls-listening", "e3-ls-visual"], "sections": [{"title": "Listening and spoken language", "concepts": ["e3-ls-listening"], "sources": ["ARLS-2", "ARLS-4", "ARLS-5", "ARLS-6"], "points": ["<strong class=\"e2-term\">Listening and Spoken Language (LSL)</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">aims for automatic, spontaneous understanding and production of spoken language in real communication.</span>", "The deck emphasizes optimal use of amplified residual hearing and full-time, consistent amplification. Speechreading appears in its broad LSL description; the specific approaches below differ in their use of visual information.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Auditory verbal therapy</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">develops spoken language through listening, following the stages and sequence of typical development.</span>", "It combines active audiological management with caregiver participation in clinic or home. Parents practice communication in daily routines. The lecture links these skills with mainstream schooling and participation in the hearing community.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Auditory Verbal Education (AVEd)</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">provides a continuum of services under one roof with specially trained staff.</span>", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Auditory oral teaching</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">uses amplified hearing together with speechreading, natural gestures, and visual cues to support spoken language.</span>", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>A child watches the speaker’s mouth and uses natural gestures while listening through amplification. That description fits auditory oral teaching. Active caregiver participation is especially emphasized in auditory verbal therapy.</p></aside>"]}, {"title": "Visual systems and family choices", "concepts": ["e3-ls-visual"], "sources": ["ARLS-7", "ARLS-8", "ARLS-9", "ARLS-10", "ARLS-11"], "points": ["<strong class=\"e2-term\">Cued Speech</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">combines phonemically based hand cues with visible speech to distinguish sounds within the same viseme group.</span>", "The talker speaks while cueing. The hand signals are not interpretable by themselves; they supplement speechreading. The slide reports adaptation to more than 60 languages. Open slide 7 below to inspect its actual cue configuration diagram.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">American Sign Language (ASL)</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">is the signed language used in the lecture’s manual visual approach.</span>", "Access to adults fluent in ASL is central. Amplification and cochlear implants are not critical to accessing this visual language. The bilingual-bicultural approach teaches ASL as the first language and English as a second language in written form.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Total Communication</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">combines communication tools through an English-based sign system, potentially including speech, speechreading, fingerspelling, natural gestures, and residual hearing.</span>", "The lecture describes a bridge toward spoken language, communication with family and peers, and participation in hearing and Deaf communities. Signing Exact English (SEE II) follows English word order. Keep this English-based system distinct from ASL.", "Choosing an approach depends on the child and family. Consider the child’s best interests, participation in the family unit, and language, thinking, and learning skills for school. The deck describes benefits of oral communication for some children and auditory access through implants; it does not establish one approach for every child.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>ASL, an English-based sign system, and Cued Speech have different relationships to spoken English. A visible hand movement does not make them the same communication approach.</p></aside>"]}], "figures": [], "studyOnly": true}, {"id": "language-development", "title": "Language: form, content, and use", "summary": "Connect access to language with structure, meaning, and successful interaction.", "part": "Language and Speech · Chapter 6", "sectionId": "e3-language-development", "concepts": ["e3-ls-language", "e3-ls-pragmatics"], "sections": [{"title": "Access, form, and content", "concepts": ["e3-ls-language"], "sources": ["ARLS-13", "ARLS-14", "ARLS-15", "ARLS-16", "ARLS-17", "ARLS-18", "ARLS-19", "ARLS-20"], "points": ["Language develops through meaningful interaction in natural contexts. Children encounter rule systems together while conversing repeatedly with adults. Inconsistent language use, obstacles to access, and incomplete exposure to spoken language can interfere with that process.", "The slide names degree of hearing loss, age of onset, and additional disabilities as major influences. It reports that 40% of children with hearing loss have other special needs. This is the lecture’s figure, not a prediction about an individual child.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Form</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">is the structure of language: syntax and morphology.</span>", "The described profile includes short, simple sentences; overuse of nouns; fewer adverbs, pronouns, and prepositions; omitted function words; and sparse or missing morphemes. In “The cat was chased by the dog,” the dog is the actor. Interpreting it as “The cat chased the dog” reverses the roles. Explicitly teach and practice the structures that change meaning.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Content</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">is vocabulary and semantics: words and their meanings.</span>", "A child may know common words but have a whole domain of missing vocabulary, such as outer space. Difficulties may include synonyms, antonyms, idioms, multiple meanings, and abstract words. Knowing “bank” as a building does not establish understanding of a riverbank.", "Parent think-alouds, repeated exposure to new words, and varied vocabulary give meaningful opportunities to expand content. These profiles guide assessment; they do not establish that every deaf or hard-of-hearing child has each difficulty.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>Omitting a plural ending concerns form. Knowing one meaning of a word but missing its other meaning concerns content. Teach the specific missing relationship rather than labeling both problems “small vocabulary.”</p></aside>"]}, {"title": "Pragmatics and communication repair", "concepts": ["e3-ls-pragmatics"], "sources": ["ARLS-21", "ARLS-22", "ARLS-23", "ARLT-repair"], "points": ["<strong class=\"e2-term\">Use, or pragmatics</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">is language used for a goal or purpose in interaction.</span>", "The lecture describes difficulty asking appropriate questions, initiating interaction, repairing a communication breakdown, and taking turns. A child may abandon an exchange when a partner misunderstands rather than clarify the message.", "Possible contributors include unfamiliar language structures, reduced vocabulary, few conversational partners, limited access to overheard conversation, and insufficient instruction in communication rules. Limited opportunity to practice reciprocal conversation matters as well as knowledge of individual words.", "Coach parents, monitor social communication, and explain the effect of noise on communication success. The transcript discusses personal FM systems as a way to improve access to the intended speaker in noise.", "Illustrative repair: a partner gives a blue block after a request for red. The child can repeat or clarify “the red one,” point, or ask for help. Model a successful exchange and practice with different partners.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>Turn-taking, initiation, and repair are pragmatic targets. A longer vocabulary list does not directly demonstrate successful repair after a misunderstanding.</p></aside>"]}], "figures": [], "studyOnly": true}, {"id": "language-assessment", "title": "Language assessment", "summary": "Use formal measures, developmental scales, and samples without losing score validity.", "part": "Language and Speech · Chapter 6", "sectionId": "e3-language-assessment", "concepts": ["e3-ls-measures", "e3-ls-conditions"], "sections": [{"title": "Formal measures and language samples", "concepts": ["e3-ls-measures"], "sources": ["ARLS-24", "ARLS-26", "ARLS-27", "ARLS-28", "ARLS-29", "ARLS-30", "ARLT-scales"], "points": ["Assessment determines the need for intervention, develops intervention goals, and evaluates progress and treatment effectiveness. Consider task type, communication mode, rapport, test procedures, and test norms.", "Tests developed for children with typical hearing are often used. The advantage is comparison with peers in mainstream environments; the difficulty is that test items and access demands can disadvantage the child. Interpret what the task actually required.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Criterion-referenced assessment</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">describes demonstrated skills against developmental criteria rather than producing a comparison with a normative group.</span>", "In the transcript, the Rossetti Infant-Toddler Language Scale and Integrated Scale of Development are discussed as developmental scales. The Integrated Scale includes listening, receptive and expressive language, speech, cognition, and social communication. A child may have different strengths across domains. Compare hearing experience, chronological age, and emerging skills without treating them as interchangeable.", "The young-child list on slide 27 includes Rossetti, Integrated Scale of Development, Preschool Language Scale-5 (PLS-5), MacArthur-Bates Communicative Development Inventories (CDI), and CELF Preschool-3. They are not all the same type of measure.", "The school-age list includes Oral and Written Language Scales (OWLS), CELF-5, Expressive Vocabulary Test (EVT), and Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test (PPVT). Vocabulary measures sample a narrower area than a broad language battery.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">A language sample</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">shows language used in spontaneous or structured communication, such as play or story retelling.</span>", "Compare the sample with formal results. In the transcript, the instructor uses morphological analysis, including Brown’s stages, to identify structures used or missing in spontaneous language. A sample can support a test result or reveal a mismatch requiring interpretation.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>Keep developmental criteria, standardized comparisons, and spontaneous performance separate. A formal score and a language sample answer related but different questions.</p></aside>"]}, {"title": "Testing conditions and interpretation", "concepts": ["e3-ls-conditions"], "sources": ["ARLS-31", "ARLS-32", "ARLT-standardization"], "points": ["Check technology before assessment. Reduce noise and distractions, build rapport, and use an assessment approach appropriate to the child’s primary communication mode.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Standardized administration</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">preserves the procedures on which the test’s scoring and normative comparisons depend.</span>", "The instructor’s example separates an original item from a later probe: a child misses an orally presented item, then succeeds when an extra gesture or sign is provided. Preserve the original score; describe the supported response as qualitative information for interpretation and treatment planning.", "Appropriate communication access and valid score interpretation must be considered together. Do not silently change a standardized procedure and interpret the result as if it were unmodified. Document the mode, conditions, and any nonstandard support.", "Slide 31 refers to Table 6.2, “Assessments standardized on children with hearing loss,” but the supplied deck contains no table entries. The transcript names several measures from a displayed textbook table, with uncertain ASR spellings. The exact table is an unresolved source gap; this lesson does not turn those uncertain names into keyed questions.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>Extra support can reveal a skill the original task missed. It provides valuable clinical information, while the original standardized response remains unchanged.</p></aside>"]}], "figures": [], "studyOnly": true}, {"id": "language-speech", "title": "Speech development and assessment", "summary": "Distinguish speech errors, articulation measures, and intelligibility.", "part": "Language and Speech · Chapter 6", "sectionId": "e3-language-speech", "concepts": ["e3-ls-speech", "e3-ls-intelligibility"], "sections": [{"title": "Speech development and error patterns", "concepts": ["e3-ls-speech"], "sources": ["ARLS-35", "ARLS-36", "ARLS-37", "ARLS-38", "ARLS-39", "ARLS-40", "ARLT-early-access"], "points": ["<strong class=\"e2-term\">Speech production</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">concerns the sounds and patterns used to express a message, including articulation and broader speech systems.</span>", "The lecture places the greatest historical impact on congenital or early childhood hearing loss. It describes better speech with more residual hearing, while also noting that modern technology changes the relationship between degree of loss and speech outcomes.", "Historically, mild to moderate hearing loss often involved intelligible speech with single-consonant or blend errors. Affricates, fricatives, and blends were commonly affected. Children could resemble younger hearing children phonologically and acquire sounds in the same order. Amplification or implants provide auditory access, with conventional articulation/phonological approaches when appropriate.", "The severe-to-profound slide lists respiration, phonation, resonance, articulation, and suprasegmentals. Its approximately 20% intelligibility figure is explicitly described as extremely variable. The instructor qualifies the profile in the transcript as children who were not aided early. Do not apply that historical profile to every child with severe or profound hearing loss.", "Vowel errors listed: neutralization, diphthong/vowel confusion, prolongation, and nasalization. Consonant errors listed: voiced/voiceless confusion, substitutions, omissions, omission of consonant blends, and distortions.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>A consonant substitution, a vowel nasalization, and altered prosody describe different aspects of speech. Language form/content/use is a separate framework from these speech-production features.</p></aside>"]}, {"title": "Articulation tests and intelligibility", "concepts": ["e3-ls-intelligibility"], "sources": ["ARLS-41", "ARLS-42"], "points": ["Conventional measures listed for segmental skills include Goldman-Fristoe (GFTA-3), Test of Minimal Articulation Competence (T-MAC), and Arizona Articulation and Phonology Scale-4.", "The slide’s significant-hearing-loss list includes the Phonetic Level and Phonological Level Speech Evaluation, CID Phonetic Inventory, Speech Intelligibility Evaluation (SPINE), and Beginners’ Intelligibility Test (BIT). These are the supplied lecture’s lists, not a current test-purchase recommendation.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Speech intelligibility</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">describes how recognizable speech is to a listener.</span>", "One method records a speech sample and asks listeners to write the words heard. Calculate words accurately identified divided by words in the sample, multiplied by 100. An illustrative 15 out of 20 words yields 75%. This is not percent consonants correct.", "Alternatively, listeners hear the whole speech passage and rate how recognizable the words were. Keep a whole-passage rating distinct from a word-by-word percentage and from a sound inventory.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>An articulation test can identify a specific sound error. An intelligibility measure asks how much of the message a listener recognizes. These outcomes can provide different information about the same speaker.</p></aside>"]}], "figures": [], "studyOnly": true}, {"id": "language-intervention", "title": "Intervention and hearing experience", "summary": "Link therapy targets, implant use, and auditory feedback with communication.", "part": "Language and Speech · Chapter 6", "sectionId": "e3-language-intervention", "concepts": ["e3-ls-therapy", "e3-ls-outcomes"], "sections": [{"title": "Language and speech intervention", "concepts": ["e3-ls-therapy"], "sources": ["ARLS-18", "ARLS-20", "ARLS-23", "ARLS-33", "ARLS-43", "ARLT-soap"], "points": ["Language therapy can increase communication with parents, peers, and adults; develop complex concepts and discourse; expand vocabulary and world knowledge; strengthen self-expression, syntax, and pragmatics; and develop narrative organization.", "Match the task to the target: practice morphemes for form, multiple meanings for content, and repair or turn-taking for use. A story retell can reveal both narrative organization and sentence-level needs.", "Speech therapy goals include appropriately timed vocalizations requiring coordinated vocal-tract movements, larger phonetic and phonemic repertoires, an audition-to-production link, and improved intelligibility.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">The audition-to-production link</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">connects hearing a speech contrast with producing it.</span>", "The transcript illustrates a final-consonant problem with “soap” produced as “sew.” Check auditory discrimination of the two words and their meanings, then develop the production contrast. Do not assume that an incorrect spoken response reveals whether the child heard the contrast.", "Listening experience helps choose developmentally appropriate targets. The transcript emphasizes checking early-developing skills when hearing experience is younger than chronological age, while continuing to evaluate the child’s actual profile.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>For “soap” → “sew,” auditory discrimination and final-consonant production are related targets. A semantic, syntax, or turn-taking task would answer a different question.</p></aside>"]}, {"title": "Implant outcomes and acquired hearing loss", "concepts": ["e3-ls-outcomes"], "sources": ["ARLS-44", "ARLS-45", "ARLS-46"], "points": ["The lecture describes children implanted early as typically developing better intelligibility and segmental/suprasegmental speech than children with profound hearing loss using hearing aids. It also describes acquisition in the same sound order as children with typical hearing. These are group descriptions, not a guaranteed individual outcome.", "Factors listed are age at implantation, duration of device use, pre-implant hearing ability, consistency of use, processing strategy and map, family support, communication mode, educational placement, and cognitive skills. Duration of experience and consistency of daily use are distinct considerations.", "<strong class=\"e2-term\">Acquired hearing loss after speech develops</strong> <span class=\"e2-definition\">can gradually reduce intelligibility through reduced auditory feedback.</span>", "Effects are extremely variable. The lecture notes that hearing loss usually must be significant, especially at high frequencies, before substantial speech changes occur. Contrast maintenance of an established speech system with first-time speech acquisition after early hearing loss.", "<aside class=\"understanding-checkpoint\"><strong class=\"checkpoint-label\">Check your understanding</strong><p>Age at implantation describes when access began. Consistency describes regular use. Acquired loss affects feedback for established speech; early loss affects access during initial development.</p></aside>"]}], "figures": [], "studyOnly": true}], "conceptNames": {"e3-ls-listening": "Listening and spoken approaches", "e3-ls-visual": "Visual systems and communication choices", "e3-ls-language": "Language access, form, and content", "e3-ls-pragmatics": "Pragmatics and repair", "e3-ls-measures": "Language measures and sampling", "e3-ls-conditions": "Valid assessment conditions", "e3-ls-speech": "Speech development and errors", "e3-ls-intelligibility": "Articulation and intelligibility measures", "e3-ls-therapy": "Intervention targets", "e3-ls-outcomes": "Implant outcomes and auditory feedback"}, "items": [{"id": "e3-ls-listening-01", "c": "e3-ls-listening", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "What is the LSL goal described in the lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Spontaneous spoken communication", "ok": true}, {"t": "Accurate written translation", "ok": false}, {"t": "Independent fingerspelling", "ok": false}], "x": "LSL targets automatic, spontaneous understanding and production of spoken language in real communication.", "s": ["ARLS-4"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-listening-02", "c": "e3-ls-listening", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "Which feature is especially emphasized in auditory verbal therapy?", "o": [{"t": "Use of an English sign system", "ok": false}, {"t": "Written English as a second language", "ok": false}, {"t": "A visual-only language model", "ok": false}, {"t": "Active caregiver participation", "ok": true}], "x": "Caregivers actively participate and carry listening-based communication into everyday life.", "s": ["ARLS-5"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-listening-03", "c": "e3-ls-listening", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "A program develops spoken language through listening in the sequence of typical development. Which approach does this describe?", "o": [{"t": "Bilingual-bicultural education", "ok": false}, {"t": "Total Communication", "ok": false}, {"t": "Auditory verbal therapy", "ok": true}], "x": "Slide 5 describes listening-based development following typical stages and sequence.", "s": ["ARLS-5"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-listening-04", "c": "e3-ls-listening", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "A child uses amplified hearing, speechreading, and natural gestures during spoken-language teaching. Which approach fits?", "o": [{"t": "Cued Speech", "ok": false}, {"t": "Auditory oral", "ok": true}, {"t": "ASL-first bilingual-bicultural", "ok": false}, {"t": "English-based Total Communication", "ok": false}], "x": "Auditory oral teaching combines amplified hearing with speechreading and natural visual cues.", "s": ["ARLS-6"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-listening-05", "c": "e3-ls-listening", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "What does the lecture emphasize about amplification in LSL?", "o": [{"t": "Full-time, consistent use", "ok": true}, {"t": "Use during weekly therapy", "ok": false}, {"t": "Use during formal testing", "ok": false}], "x": "Consistent, full-time amplification supports access in everyday communication.", "s": ["ARLS-4"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-listening-06", "c": "e3-ls-listening", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "tf", "q": "Auditory Verbal Education is described as a continuum of services under one roof with specially trained staff.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": true}, {"t": "False", "ok": false}], "x": "AVEd is described this way on slide 5.", "s": ["ARLS-5"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-listening-07", "c": "e3-ls-listening", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "Which skill is a stated auditory oral goal for school integration?", "o": [{"t": "Developing spoken language and communication", "ok": true}, {"t": "Replacing speech with written English", "ok": false}, {"t": "Learning an English sign system", "ok": false}], "x": "Auditory oral goals include spoken language and communication skills for school success.", "s": ["ARLS-6"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-listening-08", "c": "e3-ls-listening", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "Which setting can be used for auditory verbal therapy in this lecture?", "o": [{"t": "A written-language laboratory", "ok": false}, {"t": "A signing residential school", "ok": false}, {"t": "A clinic or home program", "ok": true}, {"t": "A standardized test room", "ok": false}], "x": "The lecture explicitly includes clinic and home implementation, with participating caregivers.", "s": ["ARLS-5"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-visual-01", "c": "e3-ls-visual", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "In Cued Speech, what do the hand cues supplement?", "o": [{"t": "Written English", "ok": false}, {"t": "American Sign Language", "ok": false}, {"t": "Speechreading", "ok": true}], "x": "The phonemic cues work with visible speech to distinguish sounds that look alike.", "s": ["ARLS-7"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-visual-02", "c": "e3-ls-visual", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "tf", "q": "The hand signals of Cued Speech are interpretable independently of visible speech.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": false}, {"t": "False", "ok": true}], "x": "The slide says the signals are individually uninterpretable and supplement speechreading.", "s": ["ARLS-7"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-visual-03", "c": "e3-ls-visual", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "What is the language sequence in the lecture’s bilingual-bicultural approach?", "o": [{"t": "SEE II, then spoken English", "ok": false}, {"t": "Spoken English, then ASL", "ok": false}, {"t": "ASL, then written English", "ok": true}, {"t": "Written English, then Cued Speech", "ok": false}], "x": "ASL is the first language and English is taught as a second language in written form.", "s": ["ARLS-8"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-visual-04", "c": "e3-ls-visual", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "Which adult models are central to the manual visual approach described on slide 8?", "o": [{"t": "Adults trained in auditory-only tasks", "ok": false}, {"t": "Adults trained in English-based signing", "ok": false}, {"t": "Adults fluent in ASL", "ok": true}], "x": "The slide emphasizes access to adults fluent in ASL.", "s": ["ARLS-8"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-visual-05", "c": "e3-ls-visual", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "Which system follows English word order in the Total Communication discussion?", "o": [{"t": "SEE II", "ok": true}, {"t": "ASL", "ok": false}, {"t": "Cued Speech", "ok": false}], "x": "Signing Exact English is an English-based sign system; ASL is a distinct language.", "s": ["ARLS-9"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-visual-06", "c": "e3-ls-visual", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "Which approach combines speech, signs, fingerspelling, natural gestures, and residual hearing in the lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Bilingual-bicultural education", "ok": false}, {"t": "Auditory oral teaching", "ok": false}, {"t": "Auditory verbal therapy", "ok": false}, {"t": "Total Communication", "ok": true}], "x": "Total Communication combines communication tools using an English-based sign system.", "s": ["ARLS-9"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-visual-07", "c": "e3-ls-visual", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "Which question belongs in the lecture’s communication-mode decision?", "o": [{"t": "Does every child use the same approach?", "ok": false}, {"t": "Can all language be delayed until school?", "ok": false}, {"t": "Will the child feel part of the family unit?", "ok": true}], "x": "Family inclusion, the child’s interests, and readiness for school are explicit considerations.", "s": ["ARLS-11"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-visual-08", "c": "e3-ls-visual", "sec": "e3-language-modes", "topic": "language-modes", "t": "mc", "q": "Why are amplification and cochlear implants described as not critical to ASL access?", "o": [{"t": "ASL is written spoken language", "ok": false}, {"t": "ASL uses visual language", "ok": true}, {"t": "ASL uses amplified speech cues", "ok": false}], "x": "ASL is a manual visual language, supported by fluent language models.", "s": ["ARLS-8"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-language-01", "c": "e3-ls-language", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "Which set contains the three major influences on language difficulty listed in the lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Vocabulary, intelligibility, speech rate", "ok": false}, {"t": "Degree, age of onset, additional disabilities", "ok": true}, {"t": "Device type, test norms, therapy frequency", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 15 lists degree of loss, age of onset, and other disabilities.", "s": ["ARLS-15"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-language-02", "c": "e3-ls-language", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "Omitting plural endings most directly concerns which language domain?", "o": [{"t": "Content", "ok": false}, {"t": "Form", "ok": true}, {"t": "Use", "ok": false}], "x": "Morphology is part of form, the structure of language.", "s": ["ARLS-16", "ARLS-17"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-language-03", "c": "e3-ls-language", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "A child knows bank as a building but not as the side of a river. Which area needs development?", "o": [{"t": "Pragmatics", "ok": false}, {"t": "Speech resonance", "ok": false}, {"t": "Semantics", "ok": true}, {"t": "Phonation", "ok": false}], "x": "Multiple meanings are a semantic/content issue.", "s": ["ARLS-19"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-language-04", "c": "e3-ls-language", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "In “The cat was chased by the dog,” which animal performs the chasing?", "o": [{"t": "Both animals", "ok": false}, {"t": "The cat", "ok": false}, {"t": "The dog", "ok": true}], "x": "The passive construction makes the dog the actor; reversing roles misinterprets sentence structure.", "s": ["ARLS-17"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-language-05", "c": "e3-ls-language", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "Which parent activity supports the content intervention described in the lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Narrating thoughts with varied vocabulary", "ok": true}, {"t": "Practicing a fixed list without context", "ok": false}, {"t": "Replacing conversation with silent reading", "ok": false}], "x": "Think-alouds, repeated new-word exposure, and varied vocabulary support content.", "s": ["ARLS-20"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-language-06", "c": "e3-ls-language", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "Which example fits the syntax profile described in the lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Rich abstract vocabulary", "ok": false}, {"t": "Frequent complex passive sentences", "ok": false}, {"t": "Overuse of nouns with omitted function words", "ok": true}, {"t": "Accurate pronouns with varied prepositions", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 17 describes simple structures, noun overuse, and omitted function words/morphemes.", "s": ["ARLS-17"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-language-07", "c": "e3-ls-language", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "tf", "q": "Meaningful conversations in natural contexts expose children to several language rule systems together.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": true}, {"t": "False", "ok": false}], "x": "The acquisition slide emphasizes simultaneous exposure to rules through meaningful interaction.", "s": ["ARLS-13"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-language-08", "c": "e3-ls-language", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "What intervention does the lecture recommend for difficulty using specific grammatical structures?", "o": [{"t": "Passage intelligibility ratings", "ok": false}, {"t": "Device mapping", "ok": false}, {"t": "Fingerspelling speed drills", "ok": false}, {"t": "Explicit teaching and practice", "ok": true}], "x": "Slide 18 recommends explicit teaching and opportunities to practice form components.", "s": ["ARLS-18"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-pragmatics-01", "c": "e3-ls-pragmatics", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "A child leaves an interaction after being misunderstood. Which target most directly addresses the problem?", "o": [{"t": "Communication repair", "ok": true}, {"t": "Vowel production", "ok": false}, {"t": "Plural morphology", "ok": false}], "x": "Pragmatics includes recognizing and repairing communication breakdowns.", "s": ["ARLS-21", "ARLS-23"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-pragmatics-02", "c": "e3-ls-pragmatics", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "Which behavior is a pragmatic skill?", "o": [{"t": "Initiating a conversation", "ok": true}, {"t": "Producing a final fricative", "ok": false}, {"t": "Identifying a word’s synonym", "ok": false}, {"t": "Using a plural suffix", "ok": false}], "x": "Initiation concerns the use of language in interaction.", "s": ["ARLS-21"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-pragmatics-03", "c": "e3-ls-pragmatics", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "Why can few conversational partners affect pragmatics?", "o": [{"t": "They alter cochlear implant mapping", "ok": false}, {"t": "They limit reciprocal practice", "ok": true}, {"t": "They prevent all vocabulary learning", "ok": false}], "x": "The lecture links few partners with limited extensive practice using language.", "s": ["ARLS-22"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-pragmatics-04", "c": "e3-ls-pragmatics", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "Which environmental factor can limit incidental language learning?", "o": [{"t": "Repeated meaningful language models", "ok": false}, {"t": "Varied conversational settings", "ok": false}, {"t": "Restricted access to overheard conversation", "ok": true}, {"t": "Frequent interaction with fluent partners", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 22 describes missing opportunities to overhear conversations.", "s": ["ARLS-22"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-pragmatics-05", "c": "e3-ls-pragmatics", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "What parent action is recommended for pragmatic intervention?", "o": [{"t": "Score each consonant in conversation", "ok": false}, {"t": "Increase story length without interaction", "ok": false}, {"t": "Monitor social skills and model interaction", "ok": true}], "x": "Parent coaching and social-skill monitoring are explicit recommendations.", "s": ["ARLS-23"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-pragmatics-06", "c": "e3-ls-pragmatics", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "A child interrupts before a partner finishes each response. Which target fits?", "o": [{"t": "Multiple word meanings", "ok": false}, {"t": "Turn-taking", "ok": true}, {"t": "Consonant blends", "ok": false}, {"t": "Auditory closure", "ok": false}], "x": "Turn-taking is a pragmatic communication target.", "s": ["ARLS-21"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-pragmatics-07", "c": "e3-ls-pragmatics", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "tf", "q": "The lecture lists reduced vocabulary as a possible contributor to pragmatic difficulty.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": true}, {"t": "False", "ok": false}], "x": "Understanding partners’ language supports successful interaction; reduced vocabulary is listed on slide 22.", "s": ["ARLS-22"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-pragmatics-08", "c": "e3-ls-pragmatics", "sec": "e3-language-development", "topic": "language-development", "t": "mc", "q": "A parent asks why conversation is harder in a noisy room. Which teaching point fits the lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Noise changes the child’s primary language", "ok": false}, {"t": "Noise improves understanding of new words", "ok": false}, {"t": "Noise can reduce access to the spoken message", "ok": true}], "x": "The lecture recommends explaining noise and communication success to families.", "s": ["ARLS-23"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-measures-01", "c": "e3-ls-measures", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which combination gives complementary evidence of a child’s language performance?", "o": [{"t": "Two device checks and an audiogram", "ok": false}, {"t": "An intelligibility rating and a hearing screen", "ok": false}, {"t": "A formal measure and a language sample", "ok": true}], "x": "The lecture recommends combining formal testing with spontaneous or structured language sampling.", "s": ["ARLS-29", "ARLS-30"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-measures-02", "c": "e3-ls-measures", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Why can a hearing-normed language test be useful in this lecture?", "o": [{"t": "It replaces observation in conversation", "ok": false}, {"t": "It provides a comparison with mainstream peers", "ok": true}, {"t": "It eliminates communication-mode effects", "ok": false}], "x": "A listed advantage is comparison with typically hearing children in mainstream environments.", "s": ["ARLS-26"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-measures-03", "c": "e3-ls-measures", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which task can provide a structured language sample?", "o": [{"t": "Word-recognition percentage calculation", "ok": false}, {"t": "Device impedance measurement", "ok": false}, {"t": "Story retelling", "ok": true}, {"t": "Pure-tone detection", "ok": false}], "x": "Story retelling is a listed language-sampling task.", "s": ["ARLS-29", "ARLS-30"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-measures-04", "c": "e3-ls-measures", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which measure appears on the young-child list?", "o": [{"t": "Beginners’ Intelligibility Test", "ok": false}, {"t": "Arizona Articulation and Phonology Scale", "ok": false}, {"t": "Rossetti Infant-Toddler Language Scale", "ok": true}], "x": "Rossetti is on slide 27; the other choices concern speech assessment.", "s": ["ARLS-27"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-measures-05", "c": "e3-ls-measures", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which pair appears on the school-age language list?", "o": [{"t": "OWLS and CELF-5", "ok": true}, {"t": "GFTA-3 and T-MAC", "ok": false}, {"t": "CID inventory and BIT", "ok": false}, {"t": "Rossetti and Integrated Scale", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 28 includes OWLS, CELF-5, EVT, and PPVT.", "s": ["ARLS-28"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-measures-06", "c": "e3-ls-measures", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which assessment purpose concerns whether treatment is helping?", "o": [{"t": "Evaluating progress and effectiveness", "ok": true}, {"t": "Selecting the initial communication mode", "ok": false}, {"t": "Identifying the hearing-loss type", "ok": false}], "x": "Progress and effectiveness are one of the three reasons for assessment.", "s": ["ARLS-24"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-measures-07", "c": "e3-ls-measures", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "tf", "q": "Spontaneous play can be used to collect a language sample.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": true}, {"t": "False", "ok": false}], "x": "Spontaneous play is listed alongside story retelling as a sampling context.", "s": ["ARLS-29", "ARLS-30"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-measures-08", "c": "e3-ls-measures", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which question is answered by a criterion-referenced developmental scale?", "o": [{"t": "How far is the score from a normative mean?", "ok": false}, {"t": "Which developmental skills are demonstrated?", "ok": true}, {"t": "What percentile describes the child?", "ok": false}], "x": "The transcript distinguishes developmental criteria from normative standard scores.", "s": ["ARLT-scales", "ARLS-27"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-conditions-01", "c": "e3-ls-conditions", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "What should be checked before administering an assessment?", "o": [{"t": "The number of errors expected", "ok": false}, {"t": "The child’s hearing technology", "ok": true}, {"t": "The final test percentile", "ok": false}], "x": "Technology should be checked before assessment to support access.", "s": ["ARLS-32"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-conditions-02", "c": "e3-ls-conditions", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which testing environment matches the lecture’s recommendations?", "o": [{"t": "Background speech to increase task difficulty", "ok": false}, {"t": "Music throughout language testing", "ok": false}, {"t": "Reduced noise and distractions", "ok": true}, {"t": "Ordinary classroom noise without adjustment", "ok": false}], "x": "The environment should reduce noise and other distractions.", "s": ["ARLS-32"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-conditions-03", "c": "e3-ls-conditions", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "A child misses a standardized oral item, then answers correctly after an extra sign. How should the second response be used?", "o": [{"t": "As credit replacing the original response", "ok": false}, {"t": "As a new normative score", "ok": false}, {"t": "As qualitative information", "ok": true}], "x": "The instructor keeps standardized scoring separate from information obtained with added support.", "s": ["ARLT-standardization", "ARLS-32"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-conditions-04", "c": "e3-ls-conditions", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which characteristic must guide the choice of communication access during evaluation?", "o": [{"t": "The easiest mode to score", "ok": false}, {"t": "The child’s primary communication mode", "ok": true}, {"t": "The examiner’s preferred presentation mode", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 32 says evaluation should be administered in the child’s primary communication mode.", "s": ["ARLS-32"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-conditions-05", "c": "e3-ls-conditions", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Why is an unrecorded change to standardized test procedures a problem?", "o": [{"t": "It changes the child’s chronological age", "ok": false}, {"t": "It prevents observation of language", "ok": false}, {"t": "It increases the number of test items", "ok": false}, {"t": "It weakens interpretation against the test’s norms", "ok": true}], "x": "Normative interpretation depends on the procedures used to obtain the response.", "s": ["ARLS-24", "ARLS-32"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-conditions-06", "c": "e3-ls-conditions", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "tf", "q": "Success after an added gesture can provide useful therapy information even when it does not earn standardized item credit.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": true}, {"t": "False", "ok": false}], "x": "The transcript uses supported responses to understand access and plan intervention while preserving the original score.", "s": ["ARLT-standardization", "ARLS-32"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-conditions-07", "c": "e3-ls-conditions", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "A child’s main language is ASL, but an examiner evaluates language using unfamiliar oral English tasks. Which concern is most immediate?", "o": [{"t": "Excessive residual hearing", "ok": false}, {"t": "Mismatch with the communication mode", "ok": true}, {"t": "Incorrect loudness normalization", "ok": false}, {"t": "Too many narrative opportunities", "ok": false}], "x": "The assessment must account for primary communication mode and task demands.", "s": ["ARLS-24", "ARLS-32"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-conditions-08", "c": "e3-ls-conditions", "sec": "e3-language-assessment", "topic": "language-assessment", "t": "mc", "q": "Which record best preserves interpretation after extra support is tried?", "o": [{"t": "Original response plus description of added support", "ok": true}, {"t": "The supported response recorded as the original", "ok": false}, {"t": "A score without administration details", "ok": false}], "x": "Keep the standardized response and document what changed during the additional probe.", "s": ["ARLT-standardization", "ARLS-32"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-speech-01", "c": "e3-ls-speech", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "When does hearing loss have the greatest historical impact on initial speech development in this lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Congenitally or in early childhood", "ok": true}, {"t": "After fluent adult speech is established", "ok": false}, {"t": "After a brief late-adult listening task", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 35 emphasizes congenital and early childhood loss during acquisition.", "s": ["ARLS-35"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-speech-02", "c": "e3-ls-speech", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which sound types are commonly affected in the mild-to-moderate historical profile?", "o": [{"t": "Vowels, nasals, and glides", "ok": false}, {"t": "Stress, rhythm, and intonation", "ok": false}, {"t": "Affricates, fricatives, and blends", "ok": true}], "x": "Slide 36 singles out affricates, fricatives, and blends.", "s": ["ARLS-36"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-speech-03", "c": "e3-ls-speech", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "tf", "q": "The described children may acquire speech sounds in the same order as children with typical hearing.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": true}, {"t": "False", "ok": false}], "x": "The lecture describes a similar sequence, sometimes resembling a younger phonological age.", "s": ["ARLS-37"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-speech-04", "c": "e3-ls-speech", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which error belongs to the lecture’s vowel list?", "o": [{"t": "Nasalization", "ok": true}, {"t": "Omission of consonant blends", "ok": false}, {"t": "Voiced/voiceless confusion", "ok": false}], "x": "Vowel errors include neutralization, confusion, prolongation, and nasalization.", "s": ["ARLS-40"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-speech-05", "c": "e3-ls-speech", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which example is a consonant error from the lecture’s list?", "o": [{"t": "Diphthong confusion", "ok": false}, {"t": "Vowel neutralization", "ok": false}, {"t": "Voiced/voiceless confusion", "ok": true}, {"t": "Vowel prolongation", "ok": false}], "x": "Voicing confusion is listed under consonant errors.", "s": ["ARLS-40"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-speech-06", "c": "e3-ls-speech", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which system concerns voice production in the severe-to-profound profile?", "o": [{"t": "Morphology", "ok": false}, {"t": "Phonation", "ok": true}, {"t": "Semantics", "ok": false}], "x": "Phonation appears with respiration, resonance, articulation, and suprasegmentals.", "s": ["ARLS-39"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-speech-07", "c": "e3-ls-speech", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "How does the lecture describe technology’s effect on interpreting speech outcomes from hearing-loss degree?", "o": [{"t": "Degree may be less determinative with improved access", "ok": true}, {"t": "Degree now determines every speech outcome", "ok": false}, {"t": "Technology removes the need for speech assessment", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 36 cautions that improved technology changes the relationship between hearing-loss degree and speech outcomes.", "s": ["ARLS-36"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-speech-08", "c": "e3-ls-speech", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which history describes the severe-to-profound speech profile discussed by the instructor?", "o": [{"t": "Use of fluent ASL from infancy", "ok": false}, {"t": "A recent hearing-aid battery change", "ok": false}, {"t": "Lack of early aided access", "ok": true}], "x": "The transcript explicitly places that profile in children not aided early; the slide also emphasizes variability.", "s": ["ARLT-early-access", "ARLS-39"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-intelligibility-01", "c": "e3-ls-intelligibility", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "A listener recognizes 18 words in a 24-word sample. What is the word intelligibility percentage?", "o": [{"t": "90%", "ok": false}, {"t": "75%", "ok": true}, {"t": "60%", "ok": false}, {"t": "80%", "ok": false}], "x": "18 divided by 24 times 100 equals 75%. The units are recognized words, not consonants.", "s": ["ARLS-42"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-intelligibility-02", "c": "e3-ls-intelligibility", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "What is counted in the denominator for a word-transcription intelligibility percentage?", "o": [{"t": "Correct listener responses", "ok": false}, {"t": "Words in the speech sample", "ok": true}, {"t": "Consonants attempted", "ok": false}], "x": "Correctly identified words are divided by words in the sample.", "s": ["ARLS-42"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-intelligibility-03", "c": "e3-ls-intelligibility", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which alternative to word transcription is described in the lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Measuring functional hearing-aid gain", "ok": false}, {"t": "Counting grammatical morphemes", "ok": false}, {"t": "Scoring receptive vocabulary", "ok": false}, {"t": "Rating whole-passage recognizability", "ok": true}], "x": "Listeners may rate how recognizable the words in the entire passage were.", "s": ["ARLS-42"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-intelligibility-04", "c": "e3-ls-intelligibility", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which test belongs to the conventional articulation list?", "o": [{"t": "Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test", "ok": false}, {"t": "MacArthur-Bates CDI", "ok": false}, {"t": "Goldman-Fristoe (GFTA-3)", "ok": true}], "x": "GFTA-3 is on the conventional segmental speech assessment list.", "s": ["ARLS-41"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-intelligibility-05", "c": "e3-ls-intelligibility", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which measure is listed for children with significant hearing loss?", "o": [{"t": "CELF Preschool, third edition", "ok": false}, {"t": "Beginners’ Intelligibility Test (BIT)", "ok": true}, {"t": "Preschool Language Scale, fifth edition", "ok": false}, {"t": "Expressive Vocabulary Test", "ok": false}], "x": "BIT appears with CID, SPINE, and phonetic/phonological level evaluations.", "s": ["ARLS-41"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-intelligibility-06", "c": "e3-ls-intelligibility", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "Which question is most directly answered by an intelligibility measure?", "o": [{"t": "How much speech does a listener understand?", "ok": true}, {"t": "Which grammatical endings does the child use?", "ok": false}, {"t": "How many meanings of a word does the child know?", "ok": false}], "x": "Intelligibility concerns recognizability of the speech message.", "s": ["ARLS-42"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-intelligibility-07", "c": "e3-ls-intelligibility", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "tf", "q": "A score based on words correctly transcribed is the same measurement as percent consonants correct.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": false}, {"t": "False", "ok": true}], "x": "Words and consonants are different scoring units; slide 42 describes word recognition.", "s": ["ARLS-42"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-intelligibility-08", "c": "e3-ls-intelligibility", "sec": "e3-language-speech", "topic": "language-speech", "t": "mc", "q": "A child has a consonant error on an articulation test but listeners understand most conversation. What do these findings describe?", "o": [{"t": "Hearing thresholds and device consistency", "ok": false}, {"t": "Sound accuracy and message intelligibility", "ok": true}, {"t": "Vocabulary and sentence structure", "ok": false}], "x": "Specific segmental errors and overall intelligibility are related but distinct measures.", "s": ["ARLS-41", "ARLS-42"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-therapy-01", "c": "e3-ls-therapy", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which language goal concerns organizing events in a story?", "o": [{"t": "Consonant discrimination", "ok": false}, {"t": "Narrative development", "ok": true}, {"t": "Vocal timing", "ok": false}], "x": "Language goals include narrative skills and story order.", "s": ["ARLS-33"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-therapy-02", "c": "e3-ls-therapy", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which target directly expands content?", "o": [{"t": "Longer phonation time", "ok": false}, {"t": "Improved consonant voicing", "ok": false}, {"t": "Device-map adjustment", "ok": false}, {"t": "New vocabulary and world knowledge", "ok": true}], "x": "Vocabulary and world knowledge concern language content.", "s": ["ARLS-33"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-therapy-03", "c": "e3-ls-therapy", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which target belongs to the speech-development program?", "o": [{"t": "Learning synonyms and antonyms", "ok": false}, {"t": "Improving conversational turn-taking", "ok": false}, {"t": "Expanding phonetic and phonemic repertoires", "ok": true}], "x": "Slide 43 lists sound repertoire expansion as a speech goal.", "s": ["ARLS-43"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-therapy-04", "c": "e3-ls-therapy", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "A child says sew for soap. Which perception skill should be checked in the instructor’s example?", "o": [{"t": "Counting conversational turns", "ok": false}, {"t": "Discriminating the final-consonant contrast", "ok": true}, {"t": "Identifying passive sentence roles", "ok": false}, {"t": "Interpreting an idiom", "ok": false}], "x": "The instructor connects hearing the soap/sew distinction with learning to produce the final sound.", "s": ["ARLT-soap", "ARLS-43"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-therapy-05", "c": "e3-ls-therapy", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "What relationship does the audition-to-production goal address?", "o": [{"t": "Hearing speech and producing it", "ok": true}, {"t": "Reading words and defining them", "ok": false}, {"t": "Using signs and translating text", "ok": false}], "x": "The lecture explicitly links audition with speech production.", "s": ["ARLS-43"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-therapy-06", "c": "e3-ls-therapy", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which plan targets language form?", "o": [{"t": "Rate a recorded passage’s intelligibility", "ok": false}, {"t": "Train a single consonant’s articulation", "ok": false}, {"t": "Practice specific grammatical morphemes", "ok": true}], "x": "Explicit practice of syntax and morphology addresses form.", "s": ["ARLS-18"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-therapy-07", "c": "e3-ls-therapy", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "tf", "q": "Language therapy can include communication with peers as well as parents.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": true}, {"t": "False", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 33 includes parents, peers, and adults.", "s": ["ARLS-33"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-therapy-08", "c": "e3-ls-therapy", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which speech target involves coordination of several vocal-tract movements?", "o": [{"t": "Retelling story events", "ok": false}, {"t": "Appropriately timed vocalizations", "ok": true}, {"t": "Learning abstract word meanings", "ok": false}, {"t": "Initiating a request", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 43 includes timing and coordination in vocalizations.", "s": ["ARLS-43"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-outcomes-01", "c": "e3-ls-outcomes", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which factor describes when auditory access through implantation begins?", "o": [{"t": "Age at implantation", "ok": true}, {"t": "Current educational placement", "ok": false}, {"t": "Family support", "ok": false}], "x": "Age at implantation is one of the explicit factors in slide 46.", "s": ["ARLS-46"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-outcomes-02", "c": "e3-ls-outcomes", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "A child has owned an implant for two years but wears it irregularly. Which distinction matters?", "o": [{"t": "Chronological age versus test percentile", "ok": false}, {"t": "Speech resonance versus sentence syntax", "ok": false}, {"t": "Duration of experience versus consistency of use", "ok": true}], "x": "Duration and consistency are separate factors on slide 46.", "s": ["ARLS-46"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-outcomes-03", "c": "e3-ls-outcomes", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which pair contains implant-related factors from the lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Reading speed and handwriting", "ok": false}, {"t": "Processing strategy and map", "ok": true}, {"t": "Vocabulary percentile and articulation raw score", "ok": false}, {"t": "School size and family income", "ok": false}], "x": "Processing strategy and map are explicitly listed.", "s": ["ARLS-46"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-outcomes-04", "c": "e3-ls-outcomes", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "What mechanism explains gradual speech changes after significant acquired hearing loss?", "o": [{"t": "Reduced auditory feedback", "ok": true}, {"t": "Loss of all language knowledge", "ok": false}, {"t": "Immediate loss of vocal-tract movement", "ok": false}], "x": "The lecture attributes gradual intelligibility decline to loss of auditory feedback.", "s": ["ARLS-44"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-outcomes-05", "c": "e3-ls-outcomes", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "tf", "q": "Speech changes after acquired hearing loss are described as extremely variable.", "o": [{"t": "True", "ok": true}, {"t": "False", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 44 emphasizes variability and a gradual course.", "s": ["ARLS-44"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-outcomes-06", "c": "e3-ls-outcomes", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which comparison is made for children implanted early?", "o": [{"t": "Identical outcomes to children implanted much later", "ok": false}, {"t": "Better speech than profoundly deaf hearing-aid users", "ok": true}, {"t": "Better speech than every child with typical hearing", "ok": false}], "x": "Slide 45 compares early CI users with children with profound hearing loss using hearing aids.", "s": ["ARLS-45"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-outcomes-07", "c": "e3-ls-outcomes", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which non-device factor appears in the lecture’s list of influences on young CI users?", "o": [{"t": "Mapping strategy", "ok": false}, {"t": "Family support", "ok": true}, {"t": "Sound processor sensitivity", "ok": false}, {"t": "Electrode configuration", "ok": false}], "x": "Family support is listed alongside educational placement, communication mode, and cognitive skills.", "s": ["ARLS-46"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}, {"id": "e3-ls-outcomes-08", "c": "e3-ls-outcomes", "sec": "e3-language-intervention", "topic": "language-intervention", "t": "mc", "q": "Which statement distinguishes acquired from early congenital hearing loss in this lecture?", "o": [{"t": "Acquired loss always prevents first-word learning", "ok": false}, {"t": "Congenital loss always preserves early speech access", "ok": false}, {"t": "Acquired loss can disrupt feedback for established speech", "ok": true}], "x": "Acquired loss after speech develops concerns maintenance through feedback; early loss affects initial acquisition.", "s": ["ARLS-35", "ARLS-44"], "tier": 1, "exam": "exam3"}], "sourcePages": {"ARLS-1": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-1.jpg", "text": "  Language and Speech of the Deaf and Hard-of-Hearing Chapter 6\nLearning Outcomes:\nUnderstand the communication modes used with children who are deaf/HOH\nUnderstand how hearing loss can impact speech and language development\nUnderstand speech characteristics of children with hearing loss\nUnderstand language characteristics of children with hearing loss\nUnderstand how CI use can impact speech and language development\n1", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 1", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-1"}, "ARLS-2": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-2.jpg", "text": "Communication Options\nListening and Spoken Language\nAuditory Verbal Approach (or AVEd)\nOral/Auditory Oral Approach\nCued Speech\nTotal Communication\nManual Communication\nASL\nBilingual/Bicultural approach\n2", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 2", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-2"}, "ARLS-3": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-3.jpg", "text": "www.ncbegin.org\nBEGINNINGS for Parents of Children who are Deaf/Hard of Hearing\n3", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 3", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-3"}, "ARLS-4": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-4.jpg", "text": "Listening and Spoken Language (LSL)\nEmphasizes Speech Communication , optimal use of amplified residual hearing, and the development of speechreading\nFull time, consistent use of amplification is essential.\nGoal: understand and produce spoken language AUTOMATICALLY & SPONTANEOUSLY in the context of real communication\n4", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 4", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-4"}, "ARLS-5": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-5.jpg", "text": "LSL: Auditory Verbal Therapy\nDevelop spoken language through listening by following the stages and sequence of typical development\nAggressive audiological management\nImplemented in a clinic program or home setting\nParents/Caregivers are present and actively participating\nDevelop skills necessary for successful mainstreaming in school and integration into the hearing community\nAVEd\nContinuum of Services under one roof with specially trained staff\n5", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 5", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-5"}, "ARLS-6": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-6.jpg", "text": "LSL: Oral/Auditory Oral\nAn approach that teaches a child to use his/her remaining hearing through amplification and the use of speechreading/natural gestures/visual cues to aid the child’s understanding of language.\nGoal\nTo develop spoken language through listening and visual cues.\nTo develop spoken language and communication skills necessary for school success and integration into the hearing community.\n6", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 6", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-6"}, "ARLS-7": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-7.jpg", "text": "Cued speech\nUses phonemically-based hand gestures to supplement speechreading\nTalker speaks while cueing the message \nIndividually, the hand signals are uninterpretable They are used to distinguish viseme members\nHas been adapted to more than 60 languages", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 7", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-7"}, "ARLS-8": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-8.jpg", "text": "Manual Visual Systems\nAmerican Sign Language (ASL)\nAmplification and CI are not critical\nAccess to adults fluent in ASL\nBilingual-Bicultural \nChildren learn ASL as the first/primary language, and English is taught as a second language in written form\n8", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 8", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-8"}, "ARLS-9": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-9.jpg", "text": "Total Communication\nUses an English-based sign language system which can include speech, speech reading, finger spelling, natural gestures and the use of residual hearing.\nGOALS\nTo provide a bridge to the development of spoken language in the very young child.\nTo provide communication between the child and his/her family, teachers and peers using sign language.\nTo support integration into both the hearing and the Deaf communities. \nThe child develops language through speech reading, listening and exposure to a combination of speech and sign-based systems in English order (SEE II)\n9", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 9", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-9"}, "ARLS-10": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-10.jpg", "text": "Selection of a communication mode\nThere is much debate about which to choose \nUltimate decision depends upon the child \nSome evidence suggests aural/oral communication helps achieve better speech and language performance, and literacy development\nCochlear implants have provided many with ability to acquire spoken language", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 10", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-10"}, "ARLS-11": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-11.jpg", "text": "Factors to Consider When Choosing a Communication Mode\nIs the approach in the best interest of the child & family?\nDoes the approach allow the child to feel part of the family unit?\nWill the child be equipped by school age with the necessary language, thinking, and learning skills?\n11", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 11", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-11"}, "ARLS-12": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-12.jpg", "text": "Language Characteristics of Children with Hearing Loss\n12", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 12", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-12"}, "ARLS-13": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-13.jpg", "text": "Some Characteristics of Normal Language Acquisition\nA process that occurs in natural contexts when child is exposed to all rule systems simultaneously\nOnly requirement is repeated opportunities for meaningful conversation with adults – through these interactions they understand rules\nChildren are cognitively programmed to learn language\n13", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 13", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-13"}, "ARLS-14": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-14.jpg", "text": "Potential Interference with Acquisition…\nLanguage is used inconsistently by adults\nObstacles prevent full access to the language\nLack of opportunities to hear spoken language in its entirety\n14", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 14", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-14"}, "ARLS-15": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-15.jpg", "text": "3 Most Obvious Factors Affecting the Degree of Language Difficulties\nLanguage learning difficulties vary depending on:\nThe degree of hearing loss\nAge of onset\nOther disabilities present (40% of children with hearing loss have other special needs)\n15", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 15", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-15"}, "ARLS-16": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-16.jpg", "text": "Significant Hearing loss can impact development of:\nForm: syntax, morphology\nThe structure of language\nContent: vocabulary, semantics\nThe words and meaning of language\nUse: pragmatics\nThe goal or purpose of language\n16", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 16", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-16"}, "ARLS-17": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-17.jpg", "text": "Form (Syntax)\nTable 6.1\nSimple sentence structures with few words\nOveruse nouns\nRarely use adverbs, pronouns, and prepositions\nOmit function words\nRarely use or omit morphemes\nPoor understanding of various sentence structures (e.g., “The cat was chased by the dog” will be interpreted as, “The cat chased the dog.”)\n17", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 17", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-17"}, "ARLS-18": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-18.jpg", "text": "Form:\nIntervention should focus on opportunities to practice these components through explicit teaching\n18", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 18", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-18"}, "ARLS-19": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-19.jpg", "text": "Content (Semantics)\nTable 6.1  \nLimited vocabulary\nCommon, everyday words; may have gaps in their vocabularies where they don’t know words about an entire concept (ex. “outer space”)\nCannot identify synonyms, antonyms, and idioms\nUnderstanding of words limited to single meaning; e.g., “bank”, “stand”\nLearn more concrete (“chair”) than abstract words (“wise”, “sentimental”)\n19", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 19", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-19"}, "ARLS-20": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-20.jpg", "text": "Content:\nIntervention:\nParents should narrate their thought process\nRepeated exposure to new words\nVaried vocabulary\n20", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 20", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-20"}, "ARLS-21": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-21.jpg", "text": "Use (Pragmatics)\nTable 6.1\nIncorrect language use\nInappropriate asking of questions\nLack of initiation skills\nAbsence of communication breakdown repair\nPoor turn-taking\n21", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 21", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-21"}, "ARLS-22": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-22.jpg", "text": "Reasons for lack of pragmatics\nUnfamiliar with language structures\nReduced vocabulary\nFew conversational partners ( d o not receive extensive practice in using language)\nUnable to overhear conversations\nLack of instruction on rules of communicating", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 22", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-22"}, "ARLS-23": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-23.jpg", "text": "Use:\nIntervention\nCoach parents\nMonitor child’s social skills\nProvide info on noise and communication success\n23", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 23", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-23"}, "ARLS-24": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-24.jpg", "text": "Speech and language evaluation\nThree reasons for speech and language assessment:\nDetermining the need for intervention.\nDeveloping intervention goals.\nEvaluating progress and effectiveness of intervention.\nPrinciples to consider when testing the speech, language, and literacy skills of children with hearing loss:\nTask type\nMode of communication\nRapport\nTest procedures\nTest norms\n ", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 24", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-24"}, "ARLS-25": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-25.jpg", "text": "Formal Language Measures\n25", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 25", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-25"}, "ARLS-26": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-26.jpg", "text": "Assessments created for children with normal hearing are often used to evaluate children with hearing loss\nDisadvantages include difficult test items \nAdvantages include comparisons to children with normal hearing in a mainstream environment\n26", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 26", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-26"}, "ARLS-27": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-27.jpg", "text": "Assessing the young child\nThe Rossetti Infant-Toddler Language Scale\nIntegrated Scale of Development\nPreschool Language Scale-5 (PLS-5)\nThe MacArthur Bates Communicative Development Inventories (CDI)\nClinical Evaluation of Language fundamentals – Preschool 3 rd  edition (CELF-P:3)\n27", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 27", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-27"}, "ARLS-28": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-28.jpg", "text": "Assessing the school-aged child\nThe Oral and Written Language Scales (OWLS)\nClinical Evaluation of Language Fundamentals – 5 (CELF-5)\nExpressive Vocabulary Test (EVT)\nPeabody Picture Vocabulary Test (PPVT)\n28", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 28", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-28"}, "ARLS-29": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-29.jpg", "text": "Assessing language skills\nShould be a formal test  \nShould collect a spontaneous or structured language sample, using a story re-tell task or a spontaneous play session, followed by a language sample analysis to compare to formal assessments\nExample task is to point to a picture of a word after hearing it or….", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 29", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-29"}, "ARLS-30": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-30.jpg", "text": "Assessing language skills (cont.)\nStory re-tell task\nSpontaneous language samples", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 30", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-30"}, "ARLS-31": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-31.jpg", "text": "Table 6.2\nAssessments standardized on children with hearing loss\n31", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 31", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-31"}, "ARLS-32": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-32.jpg", "text": "Essential Conditions for evaluating language abilities:\nCheck technology before administering any assessments\nTest environment – reducing noise and other distractions\nAssessments should not be modified – maintain standardization\nAdministered using child’s primary communication mode\n32", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 32", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-32"}, "ARLS-33": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-33.jpg", "text": "Language therapy\nGoals of language development program can include:\nIncrease communication between parent and child as well as with peers and adults.\nPromote knowledge of complex concepts, discourse units. (stories, inferencing, etc.)\nEnhance vocabulary growth.\nIncrease world knowledge.\nEnhance self-expression.\nEnhance growth in use of language syntax and pragmatics.\nDevelop narrative skills. (order of the story)", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 33", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-33"}, "ARLS-34": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-34.jpg", "text": "SPEECH DEVELOPMENT in Children with Hearing Loss\n34", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 34", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-34"}, "ARLS-35": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-35.jpg", "text": "Speech Development cont.\nThe effects of hearing loss on speech production are most pronounced with children who have a congenital hearing loss or who lost their hearing in early childhood.\nChildren with more residual hearing speak better than children with less residual hearing\n35", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 35", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-35"}, "ARLS-36": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-36.jpg", "text": "Speech Characteristics With Hearing Loss\nSpeech Characteristics\nHistorically, children with mild to moderate hearing loss often had intelligible speech, with the predominant speech errors being either:\nthe misarticulation of single consonants or consonant blends \nSounds most commonly in error were the affricates, fricatives, and blends. \nGiven these types of errors, the use of standard articulation tests is appropriate.\nWith the advances in access to hearing technology, degree of hearing loss is often not a factor for outcomes in speech development\n36", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 36", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-36"}, "ARLS-37": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-37.jpg", "text": "Speech Characteristics with Hearing Loss\nPhonologically, these children look like normally-hearing kids of a younger age\nThese children develop and use speech sounds in the same order as normally hearing kids\n37", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 37", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-37"}, "ARLS-38": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-38.jpg", "text": "Speech Management of Hearing Loss\nAmplification/CI is a must\nArticulation and phonological management can generally be approached in the same way as a normally hearing child \n38", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 38", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-38"}, "ARLS-39": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-39.jpg", "text": "Speech Characteristics of Severe to profound hearing loss in Children\nWhat’s normal:\nNothing\nWhat’s not normal:\nRespiration, phonation, resonance, articulation, and suprasegmental\nIntelligibility averages about 20%, but extremely variable on a case-by-case basis\n39", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 39", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-39"}, "ARLS-40": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-40.jpg", "text": "Other common articulation errors\nCommon vowel errors:\nNeutralizations\nDiphthong and vowel confusion\nProlongations\nNasalizations\nCommon consonant errors:\nVoiced/voiceless confusions\nSubstitutions\nOmissions\nOmissions of consonant blends\nDistortions\n40", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 40", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-40"}, "ARLS-41": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-41.jpg", "text": "Articulation testing\nOften conventional articulation tests are used to assess segmental speech skills. Most common:\nGoldman-Fristoe (GFTA-3)\nTest of Minimal Articulation Competence (T-MAC)\nArizona Articulation and Phonology Scale- 4\nTests designed for children with significant hearing loss:\nPhonetic Level and the Phonological Level Speech Evaluation\nCID Phonetic Inventory\nSpeech Intelligibility Evaluation (SPINE)\nBeginners’ Intelligibility Test (BIT)\n41", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 41", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-41"}, "ARLS-42": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-42.jpg", "text": "Assessing articulation skills\nSpeech Assessment\nSpeech intelligibility measures are typically based on recorded speech samples, with two different ways to obtain the intelligibility rating.\nFirst, one or more listeners can be asked to write down the words they hear, and the examiner can determine the percentage of words accurately identified \nAlternatively, listeners can be asked to listen to the entire speech passage and rate how recognizable the words are in the passage", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 42", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-42"}, "ARLS-43": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-43.jpg", "text": "Speech therapy\nGoals of a speech development program can include:\nIncrease vocalizations that have appropriate timing characteristics, require several vocal tract movements.\nExpand phonetic and phonemic repertoires.\nEstablish link between audition and speech production.\nIncrease speech intelligibility.", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 43", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-43"}, "ARLS-44": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-44.jpg", "text": "Speech Characteristics of Acquired Hearing Loss\nExtremely variable effects\nUsually the degree of hearing loss has to be very significant, especially in the high frequencies\nDecline in intelligibility is gradual and due to loss of auditory feedback \n44", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 44", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-44"}, "ARLS-45": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-45.jpg", "text": "Cochlear Implants and speech production\nChildren who receive cochlear implants, especially those who receive one at an early age, typically acquire better speech intelligibility and segmental and suprasegmental speaking abilities than children who have profound hearing loss and who use hearing aids. \nTypically acquire speech sounds in the same order as children with normal hearing\n45", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 45", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-45"}, "ARLS-46": {"image": "assets/exam3/language/pages/ARLS-46.jpg", "text": "Factors that affect speech and language in young CI users\nAge of implantation\nDuration of device use\nPre-implant hearing ability\nConsistency of device use\nCI processing strategy and map\nFamily support\nCommunication mode\nEducational placement\nCognitive skills\n46", "label": "Language and Speech · slide 46", "href": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html#slide-46"}, "ARLT-repair": {"text": "Absence of communication will break down the pair so they don't understand when they're misunderstood by someone else and so So, you know, if someone gives them that racial expression, they don't know that they weren't understood and need to fix their or repair that communication breakdown.", "label": "Lecture transcript · repair", "href": "assets/exam3/language/transcript-excerpts.html#repair", "note": "Exact excerpt from the supplied automatic transcript; transcription errors are retained. Teaching resolves terminology against the slides."}, "ARLT-scales": {"text": "It doesn't compare them with standard scores. Any questions about this or want to see this?", "label": "Lecture transcript · scales", "href": "assets/exam3/language/transcript-excerpts.html#scales", "note": "Exact excerpt from the supplied automatic transcript; transcription errors are retained. Teaching resolves terminology against the slides."}, "ARLT-standardization": {"text": "you can jot that down on your um test protocol but you can't count it as a correct answer It's just going to give you lots of information that you can put in your report. The client is, you know, understands really good um gestural information. They just don't have just the oral, oral, oral um information yet.", "label": "Lecture transcript · standardization", "href": "assets/exam3/language/transcript-excerpts.html#standardization", "note": "Exact excerpt from the supplied automatic transcript; transcription errors are retained. Teaching resolves terminology against the slides."}, "ARLT-early-access": {"text": "So, speech characteristics of severe to profound hearing loss in children. And these are these are the ones that are were not aided at an early age, is what I'm", "label": "Lecture transcript · early access", "href": "assets/exam3/language/transcript-excerpts.html#early-access", "note": "Exact excerpt from the supplied automatic transcript; transcription errors are retained. Teaching resolves terminology against the slides."}, "ARLT-soap": {"text": "Okay, ready? Listen. Touch soap. And then, oh, that was so, that was so, I said soap. That's this one. Soap. So you're trying to get them to To discriminate between that final consonant and then you want them to produce it as well. So we're working on P, final P. Can they hear the difference?", "label": "Lecture transcript · soap", "href": "assets/exam3/language/transcript-excerpts.html#soap", "note": "Exact excerpt from the supplied automatic transcript; transcription errors are retained. Teaching resolves terminology against the slides."}}, "sources": {"ARLS": {"t": "Language and Speech of Deaf and HOH · Chapter 6 · 2025 slides", "f": "assets/exam3/language/sources.html", "tier": 1, "page": true}, "ARLT": {"t": "Supplied lecture transcript · selected excerpts", "f": "assets/exam3/language/transcript-excerpts.html", "tier": 1, "page": false}}};var D=L.EXAM3_DATA;D.topics.push.apply(D.topics,A.topics);D.items.push.apply(D.items,A.items);Object.assign(D.conceptNames,A.conceptNames);Object.assign(D.sourcePages,A.sourcePages);Object.assign(D.sources,A.sources);Object.assign(L.SOURCES,A.sources);L.DOMAINS.e3.name='Final Exam';A.topics.forEach(function(t){L.SECTIONS.push({id:t.sectionId,n:L.SECTIONS.length+1,title:t.title,short:t.title,dom:'e3',exam:'exam3',src:t.sections[0].sources});t.concepts.forEach(function(c){L.CONCEPTS[c]={id:c,name:A.conceptNames[c],sec:t.sectionId};});L.GUIDE[t.sectionId]=[{id:t.sectionId+'-guide',h:t.title,tier:1,c:t.concepts,html:t.sections.map(function(s){return '<h3>'+s.title+'</h3><ul>'+s.points.map(function(p){return '<li>'+p+'</li>';}).join('')+'</ul>';}).join(''),src:t.sections.flatMap(function(s){return s.sources;})}];});L.ITEMS=L.ITEMS.concat(A.items);D.missing=D.missing.filter(function(s){return !s.includes('coverage beyond');});D.missing.push('Only Deaf Culture and Language and Speech are integrated. Additional final-exam material and new Exam focus coverage are deferred.','The actual Table 6.2 is absent from the supplied language deck.');})(typeof window!=='undefined'?window:globalThis);
